        "id": "what-obligations-does-the-cayman-company-carry-after-listing",
        "question": "What obligations does the Cayman company carry after listing?",
        "answerHtml": "<p>On the Cayman side, remarkably few — listing on an international exchange does not impose additional Cayman Islands obligations. The company maintains its registered office and licensed local agent, files an annual return, pays annual government fees, keeps its beneficial-ownership register current under the 2024 amendments, and submits its economic-substance notification; there is no requirement to hold annual general meetings in the territory and no Cayman-imposed interim reporting for a NASDAQ or NYSE issuer. Annual maintenance for a typical holding company runs on the order of $5,000–9,500. The real ongoing regime is the U.S. one: Form 20-F annually, Form 6-K for material announcements, Sarbanes-Oxley certifications and internal-control requirements, Schedule 13D/13G beneficial-ownership filings by holders, and the exchange's continued listing standards. In practice a Cayman FPI carries a lighter combined compliance load than a comparable Delaware issuer.</p><p><em>Related topics:</em> <a href=\"/products/nasdaq-direct-listing\">NASDAQ Direct Listing</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products/nyse-direct-listing\">NYSE Direct Listing</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products/reg-s\">Regulation S</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products\">Our Product Line</a></p>",
