        "id": "what-taxes-does-a-cayman-islands-company-pay",
        "question": "What taxes does a Cayman Islands company pay?",
        "answerHtml": "<p>None at the Cayman level on international operations: no corporate tax, no income tax, no capital-gains tax, and no withholding tax on dividends or interest paid out. A company can also obtain a government undertaking preserving that tax-neutral treatment for 20 years, so a future change in Cayman law cannot reach it during the term. Two clarifications matter. First, tax-neutral is not tax-free for shareholders — U.S. and other investors still pay tax at home on their gains and dividends under their own law, and the group's operating subsidiaries pay tax where they actually do business; the Cayman layer simply adds no second charge at the parent. Second, the jurisdiction runs a transparent regime: automatic exchange of information with tax authorities, an economic-substance law for relevant activities, and an exit from the FATF grey list in October 2023. The structure is about neutrality and predictability, not concealment — a distinction U.S. investors and the <a href=\"/products/nasdaq-direct-listing\">exchanges</a> understand well.</p><p><em>Related topics:</em> <a href=\"/products/nasdaq-direct-listing\">NASDAQ Direct Listing</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products/nyse-direct-listing\">NYSE Direct Listing</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products/reg-s\">Regulation S</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products\">Our Product Line</a></p>",
