        "answerHtml": "<p>Yes — the structure is a chassis for the same twelve routes on this page. Before listing, the Cayman parent can run a <a href=\"/products/reg-s\">Regulation S</a> offshore tranche to international investors — often Category 1, with no distribution compliance period, where there is no substantial U.S. market interest — alongside a <a href=\"/products/reg-d-506b\">Reg D</a> placement to U.S. accredited investors. At listing, the F-1 can register a primary raise, or the company can list secondary with no raise at all — the Spotify model, on Form F-1, no underwriters. After listing, an <a href=\"/products/eloc\">ELOC</a> provides committed standby capital and a <a href=\"/products/pipe\">PIPE</a> places institutional blocks, and Form F-3 shelf eligibility arrives after twelve months of timely reporting. The Cayman layer changes the forms and the governance regime, not the capital toolkit.</p><p><em>Related topics:</em> <a href=\"/products/nasdaq-direct-listing\">NASDAQ Direct Listing</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products/nyse-direct-listing\">NYSE Direct Listing</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products/reg-s\">Regulation S</a> &middot; <a href=\"/products\">Our Product Line</a></p>",
